WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:05.333 --> 00:00:08.006
Hello, hello, hello, and welcome

00:00:08.008 --> 00:00:09.835
to English Learning for Curious

00:00:09.836 --> 00:00:12.481
Minds, by Leonardo English, the show

00:00:12.490 --> 00:00:14.254
where you can listen to fascinating

00:00:14.255 --> 00:00:16.779
stories and learn weird and wonderful

00:00:16.791 --> 00:00:18.699
things about the world at the same

00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:20.344
time as improving your English.

00:00:20.721 --> 00:00:23.908
I'm Alastair Budge, and today we are going

00:00:23.919 --> 00:00:26.463
to be talking about the science of memory.

00:00:26.863 --> 00:00:29.531
We'll meet a man who lost the ability

00:00:29.531 --> 00:00:32.200
to remember, find out why your brain

00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:34.955
is built to forget, ask whether you

00:00:34.956 --> 00:00:37.479
can really train your memory, and look

00:00:37.484 --> 00:00:39.852
at how that might apply, or might not

00:00:39.854 --> 00:00:42.616
apply, to learning a new language.

00:00:42.941 --> 00:00:45.186
OK then, let's not waste a

00:00:45.193 --> 00:00:47.614
minute and get right into it.

00:00:48.872 --> 00:00:53.707
It is September of 1953, and in a hospital

00:00:53.859 --> 00:00:56.503
in Hartford, Connecticut, a young man

00:00:56.738 --> 00:00:59.576
is being prepared for brain surgery.

00:01:00.208 --> 00:01:02.274
His name is Henry Molaison.

00:01:02.865 --> 00:01:05.866
He is 27 years old, polite, good

00:01:05.866 --> 00:01:08.299
with numbers, and desperately ill.

00:01:09.335 --> 00:01:11.924
Henry has severe epilepsy.

00:01:12.547 --> 00:01:15.010
He has suffered from seizures since

00:01:15.010 --> 00:01:17.954
he was a boy, and by now they are so

00:01:17.977 --> 00:01:20.598
frequent and so violent that he cannot

00:01:20.599 --> 00:01:23.531
live any semblance of a normal life.

00:01:24.293 --> 00:01:26.434
He has tried medication, but

00:01:26.649 --> 00:01:28.508
it has done almost nothing.

00:01:29.208 --> 00:01:31.455
So his surgeon, a confident

00:01:31.457 --> 00:01:33.489
man named William Scoville,

00:01:33.794 --> 00:01:35.752
proposes something radical.

00:01:36.432 --> 00:01:39.295
He will open Henry's skull and

00:01:39.305 --> 00:01:41.827
remove the part of the brain where

00:01:41.829 --> 00:01:44.539
he believes the seizures begin.

00:01:45.539 --> 00:01:47.612
Specifically, he will remove a

00:01:47.619 --> 00:01:50.092
small, curved structure that sits

00:01:50.125 --> 00:01:52.131
deep inside each side of the brain.

00:01:52.835 --> 00:01:55.392
It is called the hippocampus, from

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:57.949
the Greek word for seahorse, because

00:01:58.219 --> 00:01:59.949
that is roughly what it looks like.

00:02:01.196 --> 00:02:03.891
What the hippocampus does, back then

00:02:03.892 --> 00:02:05.856
at least, was a bit of a mystery.

00:02:06.296 --> 00:02:09.882
Still, in 1953, this is not considered

00:02:09.912 --> 00:02:11.885
reason enough to leave it alone.

00:02:12.919 --> 00:02:15.626
The operation, in one sense, works.

00:02:15.910 --> 00:02:18.141
The doctor opens up young Henry,

00:02:18.321 --> 00:02:20.544
removes the hippocampus, and

00:02:20.547 --> 00:02:22.273
sews everything back together.

00:02:23.319 --> 00:02:25.988
Henry recovers well, he can walk and

00:02:25.989 --> 00:02:28.554
talk, his intelligence is untouched,

00:02:28.654 --> 00:02:31.535
and his personality is the same as ever.

00:02:32.459 --> 00:02:34.477
And importantly, the seizures

00:02:34.708 --> 00:02:36.500
become much less severe.

00:02:37.461 --> 00:02:40.179
But it soon becomes clear that

00:02:40.179 --> 00:02:41.851
something is terribly wrong.

00:02:42.664 --> 00:02:44.812
Henry can remember his childhood.

00:02:45.105 --> 00:02:47.186
He can remember his parents, the

00:02:47.186 --> 00:02:48.876
Great Depression, and the war.

00:02:49.738 --> 00:02:52.131
What he cannot do is

00:02:52.133 --> 00:02:54.627
make a single new memory.

00:02:55.512 --> 00:02:57.419
A doctor comes into his room,

00:02:57.741 --> 00:02:59.539
introduces herself, chats with

00:02:59.539 --> 00:03:01.558
him for half an hour, and leaves.

00:03:02.258 --> 00:03:04.314
Five minutes later she returns,

00:03:04.552 --> 00:03:06.327
and for Henry, it is the

00:03:06.339 --> 00:03:07.984
first time they have ever met.

00:03:08.977 --> 00:03:11.621
Henry can still hold a conversation.

00:03:11.933 --> 00:03:13.763
He can keep a number in his head

00:03:14.018 --> 00:03:15.822
for about half a minute, as long as

00:03:15.823 --> 00:03:17.654
he keeps repeating it to himself.

00:03:18.733 --> 00:03:20.230
The moment his attention

00:03:20.230 --> 00:03:22.142
moves on, it is gone.

00:03:23.332 --> 00:03:25.973
He reads the same magazines again

00:03:26.230 --> 00:03:27.926
and again, because he has no

00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:30.161
recollection of ever having read them.

00:03:30.759 --> 00:03:32.622
He eats lunch, and twenty minutes

00:03:32.624 --> 00:03:35.367
later not only can he not tell you what

00:03:35.379 --> 00:03:37.913
he had for lunch, he cannot say for

00:03:37.914 --> 00:03:40.193
certain whether he has even had it.

00:03:41.192 --> 00:03:43.664
Every time he is told that his uncle

00:03:43.664 --> 00:03:46.138
has died, he breaks down in tears

00:03:46.462 --> 00:03:48.586
as if hearing it for the first time.

00:03:49.861 --> 00:03:51.627
Henry Molaison lived like

00:03:51.627 --> 00:03:54.247
this for the next 55 years.

00:03:54.746 --> 00:03:56.585
Scientists studied him for

00:03:56.585 --> 00:03:58.571
the rest of his life, and he

00:03:58.611 --> 00:04:00.170
could not remember any of them.

00:04:01.193 --> 00:04:04.592
When he died in 2008, the world

00:04:04.862 --> 00:04:06.507
finally learnt his full name:

00:04:07.001 --> 00:04:08.656
he had been known in hundreds of

00:04:08.657 --> 00:04:11.982
scientific papers simply as H.M.

00:04:13.264 --> 00:04:16.954
It sounds like a tragedy, and it was.

00:04:17.274 --> 00:04:20.119
But what happened to Henry probably

00:04:20.119 --> 00:04:22.285
taught us more about memory than

00:04:22.292 --> 00:04:24.760
almost any other experiment in history.

00:04:25.707 --> 00:04:28.780
Before Henry, many scientists believed

00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:32.171
that memory was spread evenly throughout

00:04:32.183 --> 00:04:35.840
the brain, like a song sung by a choir.

00:04:36.232 --> 00:04:37.755
Every singer knows every song.

00:04:38.154 --> 00:04:39.987
Take away a few singers, and

00:04:39.990 --> 00:04:41.866
you can still hear every song,

00:04:42.215 --> 00:04:43.808
just a little more faintly.

00:04:44.698 --> 00:04:46.746
Remove a bit of the brain, and you

00:04:46.748 --> 00:04:48.166
would lose a bit of everything.

00:04:49.355 --> 00:04:51.953
Henry proved this was wrong.

00:04:52.609 --> 00:04:55.566
Memory depended on specific

00:04:55.577 --> 00:04:58.085
structures, and the hippocampus was

00:04:58.094 --> 00:05:00.325
clearly one of the most important.

00:05:00.870 --> 00:05:04.020
Without it, new experiences simply could

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:06.359
not be turned into lasting memories.

00:05:07.817 --> 00:05:09.375
And there was a second,

00:05:10.067 --> 00:05:11.226
stranger discovery.

00:05:12.061 --> 00:05:14.797
A young researcher called Brenda

00:05:14.844 --> 00:05:18.052
Milner gave Henry a drawing task.

00:05:18.811 --> 00:05:21.915
He had to trace a star shape while

00:05:21.915 --> 00:05:24.189
looking only at his hand in the mirror.

00:05:24.229 --> 00:05:26.501
It might sound easy, but

00:05:26.539 --> 00:05:27.958
it's surprisingly difficult.

00:05:28.467 --> 00:05:28.991
I promise.

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:31.055
I tried it because I too thought it

00:05:31.056 --> 00:05:33.136
sounded easy, but it really isn't.

00:05:34.199 --> 00:05:36.749
Henry was terrible at it, as

00:05:36.906 --> 00:05:38.306
everyone is in the beginning.

00:05:39.010 --> 00:05:42.439
But day after day, he got better, at

00:05:42.440 --> 00:05:44.837
exactly the rate a healthy person would.

00:05:45.490 --> 00:05:48.928
And every single day, he insisted he had

00:05:48.930 --> 00:05:51.624
never seen the task before in his life.

00:05:52.388 --> 00:05:55.378
His hands had learnt the skill, but

00:05:55.607 --> 00:05:58.020
he had no memory of learning it.

00:05:58.888 --> 00:06:01.791
So, this tells us something important:

00:06:02.116 --> 00:06:05.066
memory is not one single thing.

00:06:05.406 --> 00:06:08.148
There is the memory for facts and

00:06:08.157 --> 00:06:10.727
events, the kind Henry Molaison lost.

00:06:11.178 --> 00:06:14.279
And there is the memory for skills, for

00:06:14.611 --> 00:06:17.550
riding a bicycle or typing or tracing

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:19.859
a star in the mirror, which lives in

00:06:19.919 --> 00:06:22.283
different parts of the brain entirely.

00:06:23.276 --> 00:06:25.274
Now, let's talk about what is

00:06:25.775 --> 00:06:27.788
actually happening in your head

00:06:28.034 --> 00:06:29.260
when you remember something.

00:06:30.272 --> 00:06:32.246
When you experience something,

00:06:32.671 --> 00:06:34.642
your brain does not file it

00:06:34.692 --> 00:06:36.595
away like a video recording.

00:06:37.257 --> 00:06:39.936
Instead, groups of brain cells,

00:06:39.987 --> 00:06:42.337
called neurons, fire together

00:06:42.520 --> 00:06:43.913
in a particular pattern.

00:06:44.740 --> 00:06:47.587
Remembering is, roughly speaking, the

00:06:47.588 --> 00:06:50.686
brain recreating that pattern later on.

00:06:52.089 --> 00:06:53.835
The hippocampus acts

00:06:53.882 --> 00:06:55.376
as a kind of director.

00:06:55.697 --> 00:06:59.073
It binds together the sights, sounds and

00:06:59.083 --> 00:07:01.550
feelings of an experience, and then, over

00:07:01.552 --> 00:07:04.398
time, it helps transfer the important bits

00:07:04.778 --> 00:07:07.472
into long-term storage across the brain.

00:07:08.471 --> 00:07:09.734
A lot of that transfer

00:07:09.979 --> 00:07:11.533
happens while you sleep.

00:07:12.181 --> 00:07:14.855
When you sleep, your brain replays the

00:07:14.856 --> 00:07:17.362
day's experiences, strengthening some

00:07:17.363 --> 00:07:19.959
connections and letting others fade.

00:07:20.749 --> 00:07:23.003
This is one reason, it's thought,

00:07:23.385 --> 00:07:26.012
why students who sleep well before an

00:07:26.013 --> 00:07:29.154
exam tend to do better than students

00:07:29.154 --> 00:07:30.972
who stay up all night studying.

00:07:32.281 --> 00:07:34.752
And here is the remarkable thing:

00:07:35.121 --> 00:07:38.024
this system physically changes

00:07:38.024 --> 00:07:40.528
shape depending on how you use it.

00:07:41.664 --> 00:07:43.499
The best evidence for this

00:07:43.726 --> 00:07:46.125
comes from London taxi drivers.

00:07:46.952 --> 00:07:49.333
To drive a traditional black cab in

00:07:49.333 --> 00:07:52.017
London, not your run-of-the-mill Uber,

00:07:52.369 --> 00:07:55.453
you must pass a test called The Knowledge.

00:07:56.315 --> 00:07:59.628
This requires you to memorise around

00:07:59.723 --> 00:08:04.076
25,000 streets and thousands of landmarks.

00:08:04.712 --> 00:08:07.124
It typically takes three to four

00:08:07.125 --> 00:08:09.691
years of study, and many people fail.

00:08:10.304 --> 00:08:13.424
In the year 2000, researchers at

00:08:13.439 --> 00:08:15.966
University College London scanned

00:08:15.976 --> 00:08:17.894
the brains of these taxi drivers.

00:08:18.450 --> 00:08:20.642
The back part of their hippocampus

00:08:20.891 --> 00:08:22.755
was larger than average.

00:08:23.253 --> 00:08:25.839
And the longer a driver had been

00:08:25.840 --> 00:08:28.288
doing the job, the larger it was.

00:08:29.170 --> 00:08:31.383
But that raised an obvious question.

00:08:32.116 --> 00:08:34.863
Perhaps people with a larger hippocampus

00:08:35.227 --> 00:08:36.591
were simply better at learning

00:08:36.591 --> 00:08:39.371
the map, and more likely to become

00:08:39.371 --> 00:08:40.980
taxi drivers in the first place.

00:08:41.731 --> 00:08:45.573
So in 2011, the same team

00:08:45.862 --> 00:08:48.785
followed a group of trainees over

00:08:48.787 --> 00:08:50.145
the years they spent studying.

00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:53.313
The ones who passed The Knowledge

00:08:53.597 --> 00:08:56.000
showed growth in their hippocampus.

00:08:56.462 --> 00:08:59.631
The ones who failed, or gave up, did not.

00:09:00.501 --> 00:09:03.680
Their brains had grown to fit the map,

00:09:04.056 --> 00:09:06.002
in the same way that someone who lifts

00:09:06.029 --> 00:09:08.673
weights every day gets bigger biceps.

00:09:09.767 --> 00:09:13.293
So if memory is this powerful, and this

00:09:13.543 --> 00:09:17.161
adaptable, why does it so often fail us?

00:09:17.486 --> 00:09:19.601
Why do you walk into a room

00:09:19.762 --> 00:09:21.225
and forget why you came in?

00:09:21.551 --> 00:09:23.360
Why can you not remember the name

00:09:23.362 --> 00:09:25.072
of someone you met an hour ago?

00:09:26.562 --> 00:09:28.050
The first person to study

00:09:28.051 --> 00:09:30.683
this scientifically was a late

00:09:30.894 --> 00:09:33.176
19th-century German psychologist

00:09:33.543 --> 00:09:35.158
called Hermann Ebbinghaus.

00:09:36.357 --> 00:09:39.143
Ebbinghaus had no laboratory and no

00:09:39.154 --> 00:09:43.177
volunteers, so he experimented on himself.

00:09:44.052 --> 00:09:46.657
He invented thousands of nonsense

00:09:46.672 --> 00:09:49.452
syllables, meaningless combinations of

00:09:49.452 --> 00:09:53.701
letters like "ZOF" and "KEB", and he

00:09:53.702 --> 00:09:56.873
memorised list after list of them, then

00:09:57.278 --> 00:10:00.018
tested himself at different intervals.

00:10:00.709 --> 00:10:02.680
It was possibly the most boring

00:10:02.872 --> 00:10:05.000
research project ever conducted.

00:10:05.944 --> 00:10:08.364
It was also one of the most important.

00:10:09.510 --> 00:10:11.156
Ebbinghaus found that

00:10:11.176 --> 00:10:13.764
forgetting follows a curve.

00:10:14.585 --> 00:10:16.796
You lose a huge amount very quickly,

00:10:16.960 --> 00:10:19.142
more than half within the first day,

00:10:19.589 --> 00:10:21.859
and then the curve flattens out.

00:10:22.714 --> 00:10:24.594
What survives the first few days

00:10:24.959 --> 00:10:27.473
tends to stick around much longer.

00:10:28.630 --> 00:10:30.848
He also found that reviewing

00:10:30.852 --> 00:10:32.862
helps, especially when the

00:10:32.866 --> 00:10:34.368
reviews are spread out.

00:10:35.192 --> 00:10:37.295
Each time you go back over something,

00:10:37.554 --> 00:10:40.165
the memory comes back stronger, and

00:10:40.265 --> 00:10:42.132
fades more slowly the next time.

00:10:42.810 --> 00:10:44.999
Review it a few days later, then a

00:10:44.999 --> 00:10:47.342
week, then a month, and eventually

00:10:47.505 --> 00:10:49.563
it becomes almost permanent.

00:10:50.603 --> 00:10:53.836
If you've ever used a flashcard app to

00:10:53.839 --> 00:10:56.222
learn English vocabulary, and the app

00:10:56.318 --> 00:10:59.079
mysteriously decides when to show you

00:10:59.083 --> 00:11:01.936
each word, that is the ghost of Hermann

00:11:01.987 --> 00:11:04.598
Ebbinghaus, still setting the schedule

00:11:04.643 --> 00:11:06.618
almost a century and a half later.

00:11:07.618 --> 00:11:10.571
Now, it's tempting to see forgetting

00:11:10.573 --> 00:11:13.862
as a design fault, a bug, but

00:11:14.140 --> 00:11:17.323
scientists who study memory generally

00:11:17.669 --> 00:11:19.495
see it the other way around.

00:11:20.550 --> 00:11:23.401
Your brain is bombarded with information

00:11:23.693 --> 00:11:26.481
every waking second: every face on the

00:11:26.481 --> 00:11:29.104
bus, every word of every conversation,

00:11:29.323 --> 00:11:31.327
every parked car on the street.

00:11:32.076 --> 00:11:34.431
If you kept all of it, finding the

00:11:34.433 --> 00:11:37.414
useful parts would be like finding one

00:11:37.448 --> 00:11:39.616
particular grain of sand on a beach.

00:11:40.563 --> 00:11:42.913
Forgetting is the filter.

00:11:43.644 --> 00:11:46.965
The brain keeps what seems important,

00:11:47.131 --> 00:11:50.086
what is repeated, what carries emotion,

00:11:50.646 --> 00:11:52.991
and then it just discards the rest.

00:11:53.825 --> 00:11:56.299
There are, as you may know, a small

00:11:56.300 --> 00:11:58.566
number of people with a condition that

00:11:58.566 --> 00:12:00.946
lets them remember almost every day

00:12:00.946 --> 00:12:03.037
of their lives in detail, with a sort

00:12:03.041 --> 00:12:05.958
of perfect diary of their own lives.

00:12:06.974 --> 00:12:08.031
You might expect them to

00:12:08.033 --> 00:12:10.421
be extraordinarily happy or

00:12:10.661 --> 00:12:12.201
extraordinarily successful.

00:12:12.882 --> 00:12:14.841
In interviews, many describe their

00:12:14.841 --> 00:12:18.306
memory as exhausting; old arguments

00:12:18.307 --> 00:12:20.903
and embarrassments stay as fresh and

00:12:20.963 --> 00:12:23.084
as painful as the day they happened.

00:12:24.133 --> 00:12:25.707
Forgetting, these people

00:12:25.707 --> 00:12:27.829
remind us, is a kind of mercy.

00:12:28.815 --> 00:12:31.827
Even the most annoying memory failures

00:12:32.243 --> 00:12:34.600
usually have a sensible explanation.

00:12:35.556 --> 00:12:37.935
For example, you might sometimes walk

00:12:37.955 --> 00:12:40.604
into a room and forget why you came in.

00:12:41.730 --> 00:12:43.283
Psychologists have actually

00:12:43.292 --> 00:12:45.466
studied this, and the answer

00:12:45.779 --> 00:12:48.488
seems to be the doorway itself.

00:12:49.804 --> 00:12:52.795
In experiments, people carrying out simple

00:12:52.795 --> 00:12:56.211
tasks became more forgetful the moment

00:12:56.246 --> 00:12:59.410
they passed through a door, even in a

00:12:59.421 --> 00:13:01.670
virtual building on a computer screen.

00:13:02.618 --> 00:13:05.464
The brain appears to treat each room

00:13:05.773 --> 00:13:08.738
as a separate scene, filing away the

00:13:08.763 --> 00:13:11.335
old one as you cross the threshold,

00:13:11.628 --> 00:13:13.794
ready for whatever the new one brings.

00:13:14.491 --> 00:13:16.619
So when you stand in the kitchen

00:13:16.798 --> 00:13:18.424
wondering what you came for,

00:13:18.715 --> 00:13:20.184
your memory has not failed.

00:13:20.724 --> 00:13:23.240
It has just tidied up slightly too

00:13:23.254 --> 00:13:26.127
enthusiastically, like a waiter taking

00:13:26.128 --> 00:13:27.801
your plate before you have finished.

00:13:29.188 --> 00:13:31.153
There is one gap in your memory,

00:13:31.390 --> 00:13:33.586
by the way, that nothing can fill.

00:13:34.433 --> 00:13:37.379
Almost nobody can remember anything

00:13:37.638 --> 00:13:39.166
from before the age of about

00:13:39.182 --> 00:13:42.050
three, and the years up to six or

00:13:42.067 --> 00:13:44.851
seven survive only in fragments.

00:13:45.927 --> 00:13:49.329
Scientists call this infantile amnesia.

00:13:50.327 --> 00:13:52.604
The leading explanation is that the

00:13:52.667 --> 00:13:55.989
hippocampus, our director of memories, is

00:13:55.989 --> 00:13:58.495
still under construction, in development,

00:13:58.781 --> 00:14:02.003
in those years, and that without language,

00:14:02.238 --> 00:14:05.732
experiences are hard to file in a form

00:14:05.792 --> 00:14:07.932
that the adult mind can find again.

00:14:08.718 --> 00:14:12.544
A study published in 2025, however,

00:14:12.964 --> 00:14:15.402
suggests there may be more to it.

00:14:16.224 --> 00:14:19.440
Researchers at Yale scanned the brains of

00:14:19.560 --> 00:14:22.672
babies as young as one, and found that the

00:14:22.689 --> 00:14:25.838
hippocampus was already storing memories.

00:14:26.388 --> 00:14:28.440
So what this suggests is that

00:14:28.451 --> 00:14:30.850
the memories may be made, but

00:14:30.874 --> 00:14:32.630
just become impossible to find.

00:14:33.469 --> 00:14:36.335
Whatever the cause, your first steps,

00:14:36.337 --> 00:14:38.600
your first words, the moment you first

00:14:38.600 --> 00:14:41.185
saw your parents: of course all of it

00:14:41.196 --> 00:14:43.722
happened, but you have no memory of it.

00:14:44.593 --> 00:14:46.440
OK, so far we have talked

00:14:46.445 --> 00:14:47.783
about memories fading.

00:14:48.341 --> 00:14:50.406
What is perhaps more unsettling

00:14:50.654 --> 00:14:52.592
is how memories change.

00:14:53.550 --> 00:14:55.980
Remember that your brain does not store

00:14:56.032 --> 00:14:58.748
recordings; it recreates patterns.

00:14:59.377 --> 00:15:02.016
And every time you recall a memory,

00:15:02.266 --> 00:15:05.004
you are rebuilding it, and the

00:15:05.005 --> 00:15:07.880
rebuilt version can pick up errors.

00:15:08.411 --> 00:15:10.715
Details get borrowed from other

00:15:10.716 --> 00:15:12.916
memories, from photographs, from

00:15:13.168 --> 00:15:15.157
stories other people have told you.

00:15:15.819 --> 00:15:17.908
Then, to continue the analogy,

00:15:18.151 --> 00:15:20.728
the edited version gets saved

00:15:20.865 --> 00:15:22.291
in place of the original.

00:15:23.381 --> 00:15:26.366
In other words, every time you remember

00:15:26.375 --> 00:15:28.484
something, you are most probably

00:15:28.909 --> 00:15:30.853
remembering it slightly differently.

00:15:32.265 --> 00:15:34.346
The scientist who has done a lot of

00:15:34.346 --> 00:15:37.342
work to prove this is an American

00:15:37.380 --> 00:15:40.082
psychologist called Elizabeth Loftus.

00:15:41.207 --> 00:15:44.414
In one famous experiment in the 1970s,

00:15:44.878 --> 00:15:47.511
Loftus showed people films of car

00:15:47.528 --> 00:15:50.450
accidents and then asked them questions.

00:15:51.321 --> 00:15:54.240
Some were asked how fast the cars

00:15:54.271 --> 00:15:56.770
were going when they "hit" each other.

00:15:57.578 --> 00:16:00.278
Others were asked how fast the cars were

00:16:00.283 --> 00:16:03.275
going when they "smashed into" each other.

00:16:04.409 --> 00:16:06.992
As you probably know, the word "smash"

00:16:07.325 --> 00:16:09.536
has an implication of high speed,

00:16:09.881 --> 00:16:12.771
whereas "hit" doesn't; it just means to

00:16:13.002 --> 00:16:14.919
come into contact with something else.

00:16:15.719 --> 00:16:17.924
So the people were shown the

00:16:17.925 --> 00:16:20.557
same film, but asked with one

00:16:20.623 --> 00:16:22.293
different word in the question.

00:16:23.232 --> 00:16:26.378
The "smashed" group estimated higher

00:16:26.396 --> 00:16:28.793
speeds, and a week later, they

00:16:28.793 --> 00:16:30.994
were far more likely to remember

00:16:31.215 --> 00:16:33.157
seeing broken glass in the film.

00:16:34.033 --> 00:16:35.736
There was no broken glass.

00:16:36.805 --> 00:16:38.839
Loftus then went further.

00:16:39.472 --> 00:16:42.597
In a study in the 1990s, she and

00:16:42.601 --> 00:16:44.771
her colleagues convinced adult

00:16:44.780 --> 00:16:47.214
volunteers that, as children, they

00:16:47.216 --> 00:16:48.916
had once been lost in a shopping

00:16:48.916 --> 00:16:52.063
centre and rescued by a kind stranger.

00:16:52.680 --> 00:16:55.561
The event was invented; the researchers

00:16:55.561 --> 00:16:57.701
had checked with the volunteers' families.

00:16:58.854 --> 00:17:01.063
About one in four participants

00:17:01.404 --> 00:17:03.241
came to remember it anyway.

00:17:03.857 --> 00:17:06.147
Some added their own details:

00:17:06.302 --> 00:17:08.807
the old man's flannel shirt, the

00:17:08.996 --> 00:17:11.170
feeling of panic, the telling-off

00:17:11.372 --> 00:17:12.780
from their mother afterwards.

00:17:13.769 --> 00:17:15.118
They were not lying, they were not

00:17:15.578 --> 00:17:17.778
knowingly telling something false.

00:17:17.993 --> 00:17:18.898
They could see it.

00:17:19.258 --> 00:17:21.384
The memory felt exactly like a

00:17:21.384 --> 00:17:23.753
real one, because, as far as their

00:17:23.762 --> 00:17:25.809
brains were concerned, it was one.

00:17:27.284 --> 00:17:30.408
Loftus had a reason for this research.

00:17:31.568 --> 00:17:35.214
In the 1990s, some therapists were using

00:17:35.215 --> 00:17:38.277
techniques to help patients "recover"

00:17:38.601 --> 00:17:41.362
memories of childhood abuse, and some

00:17:41.383 --> 00:17:44.036
of those memories led to court cases.

00:17:45.146 --> 00:17:48.082
Her work showed how easily a false

00:17:48.082 --> 00:17:50.419
memory could be created, which, as you

00:17:50.419 --> 00:17:52.237
might imagine, made her one of the most

00:17:52.269 --> 00:17:54.486
controversial psychologists of her time.

00:17:55.771 --> 00:17:57.986
Even the people we met earlier, the

00:17:57.987 --> 00:18:00.278
ones who can remember almost every

00:18:00.278 --> 00:18:02.431
day of their lives, are open to this.

00:18:02.974 --> 00:18:04.934
When researchers tried similar

00:18:04.942 --> 00:18:08.695
experiments on them in 2013, they

00:18:08.713 --> 00:18:10.822
formed false memories at about

00:18:10.825 --> 00:18:12.608
the same rate as everyone else.

00:18:13.995 --> 00:18:16.089
And if you are thinking that this only

00:18:16.091 --> 00:18:19.544
applies to small, forgettable events,

00:18:19.794 --> 00:18:22.037
psychologists have tested that too.

00:18:23.353 --> 00:18:25.914
The day after the space shuttle Challenger

00:18:26.091 --> 00:18:29.972
exploded in 1986, a researcher named

00:18:30.030 --> 00:18:33.047
Ulric Neisser asked his students to write

00:18:33.048 --> 00:18:35.477
down exactly where they were when they

00:18:35.478 --> 00:18:37.724
heard the news: who told them, what they

00:18:37.725 --> 00:18:39.948
were doing, how they felt, and so on.

00:18:41.351 --> 00:18:43.450
Two and a half years later, he

00:18:43.452 --> 00:18:44.786
asked them the same questions.

00:18:45.727 --> 00:18:47.829
The students were describing what

00:18:47.829 --> 00:18:50.161
are called flashbulb memories, the

00:18:50.435 --> 00:18:52.819
vivid, photograph-like memories of

00:18:53.356 --> 00:18:55.576
shocking events, the kind people

00:18:55.576 --> 00:18:57.782
describe as "burned in" forever.

00:18:59.200 --> 00:19:01.682
The students' new answers were full

00:19:01.706 --> 00:19:03.974
of confidence and full of errors.

00:19:04.669 --> 00:19:06.968
Some had changed the place, the

00:19:06.968 --> 00:19:09.345
messenger, even who they were with.

00:19:10.260 --> 00:19:12.903
One student, when she was shown her own

00:19:13.183 --> 00:19:15.463
handwritten account from the day after

00:19:15.463 --> 00:19:18.273
the disaster, she looked closely at it.

00:19:18.475 --> 00:19:20.165
She agreed that it was her

00:19:20.179 --> 00:19:22.379
handwriting, but insisted that

00:19:22.380 --> 00:19:24.168
this was not what had happened.

00:19:24.740 --> 00:19:28.093
She trusted the memory over the evidence.

00:19:29.605 --> 00:19:31.835
And this, of course, has consequences

00:19:31.889 --> 00:19:34.428
far beyond the laboratory, and

00:19:34.608 --> 00:19:36.680
beyond the world of the theoretical.

00:19:37.752 --> 00:19:40.750
In a court of law, for example, most

00:19:40.773 --> 00:19:43.570
legal systems put a premium on the

00:19:43.581 --> 00:19:46.138
testimony of a confident eyewitness.

00:19:46.662 --> 00:19:48.525
A witness points at the defendant

00:19:48.567 --> 00:19:50.420
and says, "That is the man. I

00:19:50.420 --> 00:19:51.781
will never forget his face."

00:19:52.441 --> 00:19:54.915
The jury believes them, and the

00:19:54.936 --> 00:19:57.957
defendant is sent down, at least partly

00:19:58.188 --> 00:19:59.949
on the strength of this testimony.

00:20:01.233 --> 00:20:03.124
For a long time, eyewitnesses

00:20:03.193 --> 00:20:04.597
were the best evidence.

00:20:05.155 --> 00:20:07.645
But then along came DNA.

00:20:08.685 --> 00:20:12.991
And since the late 1980s, DNA evidence

00:20:13.196 --> 00:20:16.244
has been used to re-examine old

00:20:16.265 --> 00:20:18.122
convictions in the United States,

00:20:18.318 --> 00:20:20.490
and hundreds of innocent people

00:20:20.701 --> 00:20:22.466
have been released from prison.

00:20:23.809 --> 00:20:26.381
In around seven out of ten of those

00:20:26.387 --> 00:20:29.676
cases, mistaken eyewitness evidence had

00:20:29.702 --> 00:20:32.080
played a part in the original conviction.

00:20:33.255 --> 00:20:36.517
Those witnesses were rarely dishonest.

00:20:36.717 --> 00:20:37.492
They were confident,

00:20:37.761 --> 00:20:39.677
and sincere, and wrong.

00:20:40.520 --> 00:20:42.622
Their memories had been gradually

00:20:42.862 --> 00:20:46.238
rewritten over time, by stress, by

00:20:46.415 --> 00:20:48.733
photographs of suspects, and by the

00:20:48.733 --> 00:20:51.147
pressures of the investigation itself.

00:20:52.523 --> 00:20:55.031
Researchers now think that a witness's

00:20:55.097 --> 00:20:57.591
very first identification is often

00:20:57.856 --> 00:21:00.222
fairly reliable, as long as it is

00:21:00.224 --> 00:21:02.153
made quickly and without pressure.

00:21:02.743 --> 00:21:04.750
The trouble comes afterwards.

00:21:05.326 --> 00:21:07.668
Every time a witness is told they

00:21:07.862 --> 00:21:09.909
picked the right person, or goes

00:21:09.910 --> 00:21:11.580
over their story again before a

00:21:11.580 --> 00:21:13.611
trial, their confidence grows.

00:21:14.325 --> 00:21:15.820
By the time they reach the

00:21:15.824 --> 00:21:17.764
courtroom, they may be far more

00:21:17.766 --> 00:21:19.828
certain than they were on the day.

00:21:20.563 --> 00:21:23.595
So, your memory fades, and changes

00:21:23.839 --> 00:21:25.471
into something that might not have

00:21:25.534 --> 00:21:27.041
ever happened in the first place.

00:21:27.908 --> 00:21:30.658
And this brings us to perhaps

00:21:31.018 --> 00:21:32.338
the most obvious question:

00:21:32.956 --> 00:21:34.330
can we do anything about it?

00:21:34.696 --> 00:21:36.578
Can memory be trained?

00:21:37.922 --> 00:21:40.134
The ancient Greeks certainly thought so.

00:21:41.169 --> 00:21:43.527
The story goes that in the 5th century

00:21:43.551 --> 00:21:47.254
BC, a poet named Simonides was performing

00:21:47.300 --> 00:21:50.131
at a banquet when he was called outside.

00:21:51.031 --> 00:21:52.795
When he was out of the room, the

00:21:52.796 --> 00:21:55.054
roof collapsed, killing everyone

00:21:55.054 --> 00:21:57.232
inside and crushing the guests

00:21:57.478 --> 00:21:59.773
so badly that their own families

00:21:59.997 --> 00:22:01.625
could not identify the bodies.

00:22:02.419 --> 00:22:06.286
But Simonides found he could name every

00:22:06.297 --> 00:22:09.539
victim, because he could picture exactly

00:22:09.648 --> 00:22:11.465
where each guest had been sitting.

00:22:12.759 --> 00:22:16.276
From this grim discovery, he is said to

00:22:16.281 --> 00:22:18.912
have invented a technique which the Romans

00:22:18.935 --> 00:22:22.269
later called the method of loci, and

00:22:22.269 --> 00:22:25.262
which we know today as the memory palace.

00:22:26.264 --> 00:22:28.767
The idea is simple: the human

00:22:28.767 --> 00:22:30.851
brain is not great at remembering

00:22:31.178 --> 00:22:34.149
abstract information, but superb

00:22:34.346 --> 00:22:36.865
at remembering places and images.

00:22:37.402 --> 00:22:40.458
So you convert the things you want

00:22:40.518 --> 00:22:43.284
to remember into vivid images, and

00:22:43.288 --> 00:22:45.175
place them, in your imagination,

00:22:45.466 --> 00:22:46.955
along a route you know well,

00:22:47.156 --> 00:22:49.033
like the rooms of your house.

00:22:50.072 --> 00:22:52.292
If this sounds like an ancient

00:22:52.307 --> 00:22:54.569
curiosity, it might surprise you

00:22:54.806 --> 00:22:56.609
to find out that almost every

00:22:56.805 --> 00:22:59.360
modern memory champion uses it.

00:23:00.113 --> 00:23:02.231
As you may know, there are international

00:23:02.252 --> 00:23:04.985
memory championships, where competitors

00:23:05.021 --> 00:23:06.412
memorise the order of a shuffled

00:23:06.413 --> 00:23:09.414
pack of cards  in under 30 seconds,

00:23:09.467 --> 00:23:11.462
or hundreds of random numbers.

00:23:12.416 --> 00:23:15.363
Brain scans of these champions show

00:23:15.825 --> 00:23:17.525
nothing unusual about their brains.

00:23:18.139 --> 00:23:20.599
What the scans do show is that

00:23:20.607 --> 00:23:23.228
while memorising, they activate

00:23:23.229 --> 00:23:25.482
the parts of the brain associated

00:23:25.658 --> 00:23:27.845
with places and navigation.

00:23:28.803 --> 00:23:30.761
They are not remembering numbers.

00:23:30.831 --> 00:23:32.860
They are walking through imaginary

00:23:32.863 --> 00:23:35.463
buildings, filled with strange pictures.

00:23:36.999 --> 00:23:40.615
In 2005, an American journalist named

00:23:40.628 --> 00:23:43.892
Joshua Foer went to report on one of these

00:23:43.893 --> 00:23:46.893
competitions, the US Memory Championship.

00:23:47.772 --> 00:23:49.968
The competitors told him that memory

00:23:49.992 --> 00:23:52.365
was a skill, not a gift, and that

00:23:52.432 --> 00:23:54.309
anyone could do what they did.

00:23:55.165 --> 00:23:57.343
He decided to test the claim on

00:23:57.344 --> 00:24:00.392
himself, and spent a year training

00:24:00.403 --> 00:24:02.117
with the memory palace technique.

00:24:02.806 --> 00:24:04.135
The following year, he

00:24:04.136 --> 00:24:05.519
returned to the competition.

00:24:06.211 --> 00:24:08.606
And he didn't just take part; he

00:24:08.615 --> 00:24:10.989
won the US Memory Championship.

00:24:12.304 --> 00:24:13.919
Research backs this up.

00:24:14.216 --> 00:24:17.060
In a 2017 study, ordinary

00:24:17.066 --> 00:24:19.130
volunteers practised the memory

00:24:19.143 --> 00:24:21.281
palace technique for six weeks.

00:24:22.105 --> 00:24:25.161
Their scores on memory tests more than

00:24:25.169 --> 00:24:27.484
doubled, most of the improvement was

00:24:27.484 --> 00:24:29.765
still there four months later, and

00:24:30.008 --> 00:24:32.160
their brain activity had started to

00:24:32.160 --> 00:24:34.368
look more like that of the champions.

00:24:35.113 --> 00:24:37.550
Now, if you're thinking, "OK, great,

00:24:37.779 --> 00:24:39.394
maybe I can just use this memory

00:24:39.402 --> 00:24:41.003
palace technique for all of those

00:24:41.080 --> 00:24:42.832
English words I keep on forgetting",

00:24:43.659 --> 00:24:46.599
it can help, but only up to a point.

00:24:47.940 --> 00:24:50.185
These techniques are wonderful for

00:24:50.438 --> 00:24:54.043
lists, speeches, numbers and names.

00:24:54.570 --> 00:24:57.299
Turning a new word into a vivid picture

00:24:57.642 --> 00:24:59.621
can help you learn it in the first place.

00:25:00.269 --> 00:25:02.076
On its own, though, it will not

00:25:02.241 --> 00:25:04.101
keep the word in your head for long.

00:25:05.199 --> 00:25:08.171
For making words stick, the research

00:25:08.404 --> 00:25:10.276
points somewhere less glamorous.

00:25:10.974 --> 00:25:13.055
It points, in fact, straight

00:25:13.071 --> 00:25:14.707
back to Ebbinghaus, the

00:25:14.864 --> 00:25:17.426
19th-century German psychologist.

00:25:18.258 --> 00:25:20.300
Space out your reviews

00:25:20.459 --> 00:25:21.583
rather than cramming.

00:25:22.092 --> 00:25:23.353
Spaced repetition, you

00:25:23.354 --> 00:25:24.213
might have heard it called.

00:25:24.893 --> 00:25:26.856
Test yourself, because the

00:25:26.885 --> 00:25:28.936
effort of pulling a word out

00:25:28.939 --> 00:25:31.041
of your memory strengthens it

00:25:31.054 --> 00:25:32.971
far more than reading it again.

00:25:33.701 --> 00:25:35.660
Connect new words to

00:25:35.669 --> 00:25:37.598
vivid images or stories.

00:25:38.364 --> 00:25:40.584
And of course remembering a word’s

00:25:40.599 --> 00:25:43.000
meaning is only part of learning it.

00:25:43.326 --> 00:25:45.071
You also need to recognise it

00:25:45.071 --> 00:25:47.050
in speech and find it quickly

00:25:47.215 --> 00:25:48.424
when you want to say something.

00:25:49.186 --> 00:25:51.239
A memory palace can help you make

00:25:51.247 --> 00:25:53.325
the first connection; but you still

00:25:53.385 --> 00:25:55.237
need to practise using the word.

00:25:56.146 --> 00:25:58.838
And lest I forget, get enough good

00:25:58.838 --> 00:26:01.004
sleep, because that is when the day's

00:26:01.006 --> 00:26:03.148
learning is stitched into place.

00:26:03.782 --> 00:26:06.054
It's not magic, but it works.

00:26:07.375 --> 00:26:09.436
When Henry Molaison went into

00:26:09.438 --> 00:26:12.580
surgery in 1953, nobody knew

00:26:12.717 --> 00:26:13.973
what the hippocampus did.

00:26:14.708 --> 00:26:17.541
Today, scientists can watch memories

00:26:17.545 --> 00:26:20.008
forming in the brains of mice, and

00:26:20.212 --> 00:26:22.031
they have even switched individual

00:26:22.035 --> 00:26:24.926
memories on and off in the laboratory.

00:26:25.783 --> 00:26:28.051
We know far more than we did even

00:26:28.084 --> 00:26:30.492
ten years ago, and vastly more

00:26:30.762 --> 00:26:32.790
than anyone knew a century ago.

00:26:33.632 --> 00:26:35.483
And yet, a great deal about

00:26:35.485 --> 00:26:37.625
memory remains a mystery.

00:26:38.481 --> 00:26:41.637
Nobody can yet say exactly what

00:26:41.668 --> 00:26:43.237
a single memory is made of,

00:26:43.365 --> 00:26:46.120
or precisely where it is kept.

00:26:46.694 --> 00:26:49.295
Nobody fully understands why

00:26:49.334 --> 00:26:51.762
some memories last a lifetime

00:26:52.108 --> 00:26:53.720
while others are gone in minutes.

00:26:54.438 --> 00:26:57.486
And despite decades of research, there

00:26:57.486 --> 00:27:00.454
is, sadly, still no way to stop the

00:27:00.454 --> 00:27:03.107
memory loss caused by Alzheimer's disease.

00:27:04.690 --> 00:27:07.284
Every one of us carries a lifetime of

00:27:07.284 --> 00:27:10.566
memories around in our heads, and science

00:27:10.906 --> 00:27:13.236
cannot fully explain how it is done.

00:27:14.366 --> 00:27:17.324
Henry Molaison never remembered any

00:27:17.325 --> 00:27:18.913
of the scientists who studied him.

00:27:19.733 --> 00:27:22.167
After his death, his brain was

00:27:22.213 --> 00:27:24.041
carefully cut into more than

00:27:24.117 --> 00:27:26.739
two thousand thin slices, and

00:27:26.976 --> 00:27:28.790
it is still being studied today.

00:27:29.702 --> 00:27:31.424
Who knows what secrets

00:27:31.591 --> 00:27:33.117
it may yet contain…

00:27:34.579 --> 00:27:36.572
OK, then, that is it for today's

00:27:36.599 --> 00:27:39.205
episode on the science of memory.

00:27:39.571 --> 00:27:40.871
I hope it's been an interesting one

00:27:41.131 --> 00:27:42.781
and that you've learnt something new.

00:27:43.348 --> 00:27:44.772
As always, I would love to know

00:27:44.772 --> 00:27:46.117
what you thought of this episode.

00:27:46.404 --> 00:27:48.006
Let me know in the comments below,

00:27:48.286 --> 00:27:49.677
if you're listening to this somewhere

00:27:49.677 --> 00:27:51.356
where you can comment, and for the

00:27:51.360 --> 00:27:53.016
members among you, you can head right

00:27:53.026 --> 00:27:55.033
into our community forum, which is at

